#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175637 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "45ec3318-742d-582c-bdf1-d9f35f0bd99c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12561" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta),$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(r,\\theta) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____} , \\: r^2, \\: 2 r \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\ny=4{x^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx =\\dfrac1{2}X, \\qquad\ny = Z, \\qquad\nz = 2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ = X^2 + Y^2.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{2}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r \\in [0,\\infty)$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\dfrac1{2} r \\cos\\theta}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175743 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/id #uuid "6745a851-eaa4-50a0-b76a-3d847284c586" :question/math-academy-id "e-8886" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta, \\: 4\\sinh{\\phi}, \\: \\text{______} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{9}-\\dfrac{y^2}{16}+z^2=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $r^2-Z^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle r, Z\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx = 3X, \\qquad\ny = 4Z, \\qquad\nz = Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nX^2 + Y^2 - Z^2 = 1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r, \\theta, Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\nY = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \nZ = Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $r^2 - Z^2 = 1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle r, Z \\rangle = \\langle \\cosh{\\phi}, \\: \\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,4Z,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4Z,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} \\cosh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta }.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175746 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of Two Sheets"}] :question/id #uuid "2b8d5d04-51f1-5394-931a-384d88692ada" :question/math-academy-id "e-8890" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi),$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\sin\\theta, \\: 3\\cosh{\\phi} \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as $x^2 + y^2 - z^2 = -9.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $\\mathbf{f}.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola $z^2-r^2 = k^2$ can be parametrized as $\\langle z,r\\rangle = \\langle k\\cosh\\phi, k\\sinh \\phi\\rangle$* for $\\phi\\in \\mathbb R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r, \\theta, z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\qquad\n\ny = r\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \n\nz = z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-9 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola $z^2 - r^2 = 9$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta$ with the positive $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14077759/assets/4e7b508432f12b8dc98e37ae2786d220eb97bdc58fe5a29c5f7728cac46471f0.png)\n\nUsing the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n\\langle z, r \\rangle = \\langle 3\\cosh{\\phi}, \\: 3\\sinh{\\phi} \\rangle,\n\\qquad \\phi \\in \\mathbb R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in \\mathbb R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue}3\\sinh{\\phi} \\cos\\theta} .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175747 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/id #uuid "84dd50d8-12e0-51d2-90e4-4e602c2e3bb8" :question/math-academy-id "e-8891" :question/problem "$$\n\\mathbf{f}(y, z) =  \\big\\langle \\text{_____}, \\: y, \\: z \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $\\mathbf{f}(y, z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $-3x=-6y^2+3z^2.$ What is the first component of $\\mathbf{f}?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n-3x=-6y^2+3z^2 \n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\nx=2y^2-z^2.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{y}^{2}-{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y \\in (-\\infty,\\infty)$ and $z \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $\\mathbf{f}$ is ${\\color{blue} 2y^2-z^2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8191ac19-4573-52c9-bc68-084798174975" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173371} {:answer/id #uuid "9406da13-2e40-59e4-b026-1da5c137b468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173372} {:answer/id #uuid "db5dd115-ceb3-5aa0-a503-a50b9294e516" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173373} {:answer/id #uuid "30d4873e-9a13-5fe6-b2a0-8e88f0782aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173374} {:answer/id #uuid "57f7282c-aa74-56bc-bebd-3e3f3e62407c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sinh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9406da13-2e40-59e4-b026-1da5c137b468" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173372} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed71cfe-2e6e-5677-b3a0-942b5d08c97a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "736a4dfd-f4fe-5ef5-9a6f-9cd95b3ae752" :question/math-academy-id "q-104007" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4a20b8-1628-5624-9d4d-bc8bfb1abac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173377} {:answer/id #uuid "d377433c-9727-58ef-9fa6-578f12dcb1e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173378} {:answer/id #uuid "1eece7c2-fbc6-5bb0-bcd9-5180b9654e92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173379} {:answer/id #uuid "35f3a676-e9f6-5426-acab-cdf85d1b741d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sinh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173380} {:answer/id #uuid "63417fdb-5252-5595-88de-866888d6396a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cosh ϕ\\sin θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c4a20b8-1628-5624-9d4d-bc8bfb1abac2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\cosh ϕ\\cos θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173377} :answer-field/id #uuid "818d02f1-d982-5b13-8b21-52df91f0046c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c824c2a-5ae5-5a01-bf81-c7a30b4783b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-104008" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}-{y}^{2}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3X,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3X,Z,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2e1c2a5-35cf-5837-8936-351b8829b474" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173407} {:answer/id #uuid "e8834a3e-4981-56db-a311-1331172023c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173408} {:answer/id #uuid "f3c0ade1-f68d-5f11-9bec-61f3fa0cf482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2y^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}z^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173409} {:answer/id #uuid "350b7ada-87c1-525f-9fe7-801cc17ade9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{9y^{2} + 2z^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173410} {:answer/id #uuid "71d99896-f4d9-5abd-b7ba-d4cefe5e5180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9y^{2} + 2z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173411}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8834a3e-4981-56db-a311-1331172023c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2y^{2} + \\frac{2}{5}z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173408} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ae8d235-e5d3-517b-b520-ee806674a5e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "035095fb-0212-56f5-82b4-682cce36fab3" :question/math-academy-id "q-104321" :question/problem "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n5x=10{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $2{y}^{2}+\\frac{2}{5}{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384675 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eedda1f9-ce77-57d6-9281-8c958719aaca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384677} {:answer/id #uuid "59115edb-6792-5ef7-8fe9-9f8449942eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384678} {:answer/id #uuid "f4e70ade-ace3-50ca-aa34-059fe9566e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384679} {:answer/id #uuid "6aa8b30b-787c-54e6-b8fb-d7c35eb92cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384680} {:answer/id #uuid "20544442-3ee7-5fff-b6a9-9d95aa1c8890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6aa8b30b-787c-54e6-b8fb-d7c35eb92cfb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384680} :answer-field/id #uuid "8469edd9-7a38-5ef9-b5a1-64bef3b997ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3312d10b-5f81-5bae-a433-29944a392e91" :question/math-academy-id "q-164463" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{z}{2}=3{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,z=6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $6{x}^{2}+8{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f84d8e32-ed84-5c17-93bf-18460e56427b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384684} {:answer/id #uuid "9ab1950f-cf5a-587e-a616-c8399f536b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384685} {:answer/id #uuid "07a98aea-06a5-5bd7-8f26-3a07ec62bc48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384686} {:answer/id #uuid "90d08ad7-3431-563b-83ad-add8bd4fe5c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384687} {:answer/id #uuid "fd9908df-fefe-5ee2-99e9-9024a6d6fed1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384688}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ab1950f-cf5a-587e-a616-c8399f536b69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384685} :answer-field/id #uuid "44f7c18f-2e4c-5185-bd5a-c5ca02ccbfa8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384683}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bdc1e9a7-73d7-57ba-931f-f608b20a72c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164464" :question/problem "$$\nf(y,z)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,y,\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(y,z)$ above parametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}.$ What is the first component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $x,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n4x=16{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2},\\,y,\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $4{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}{z}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384689 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53dc3b84-68e3-53f9-b31d-8be0839e1a7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384691} {:answer/id #uuid "fc16b1c6-aef2-5184-9819-e8715314bf69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384692} {:answer/id #uuid "d8a81934-cd7f-52eb-a556-f8e04ef27728" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384693} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1dc630-c3fe-54a6-a22f-0f0d57e1aad5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384694} {:answer/id #uuid "1e623f68-b9f4-5534-a81c-e43e69b4c47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384695}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e623f68-b9f4-5534-a81c-e43e69b4c47f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384695} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e9ee65f-e8a3-5caf-9a58-c31be02b006f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384690}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ac591ac-50de-5acc-afeb-b5b2a30bc572" :question/math-academy-id "q-164465" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,z)=⟨x,\\,\\text{_____},\\,z⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,z)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $3y={z}^{2}-9{x}^{2}.$ What is the second component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y={z}^{2}-9{x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,z) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2},\\,z⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}-3{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "140679a6-6a14-5f92-9a92-68917f197863" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "728174b1-46c7-5dea-93dd-802074936579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384698} {:answer/id #uuid "5cdff4da-a249-5e4f-8443-a97bab00afbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384699} {:answer/id #uuid "92aaa2ee-1bbb-5125-8dce-62d2e8940f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384700} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4acb40-e3c5-5807-8d9b-9230d733443c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{y}^{2}-9{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384701} {:answer/id #uuid "91d6346c-060b-5726-9e89-8a26fe3b2651" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92aaa2ee-1bbb-5125-8dce-62d2e8940f48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384700} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f883ff0-fd67-5544-960e-419392031df4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c1244e2-f3bf-5ec0-a896-2d7fc1af0fde" :question/math-academy-id "q-164469" :question/problem "$$\nf(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $f(x,y)$ above parametrizes the hyperbolic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as $\\frac{2z}{3}=4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}.$ What is the third component of $f?$" :question/worked-solution "Solving our equation for $z,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{2z}{3}=4{x}^{2}-6{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,z=6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$   \n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $6{x}^{2}-9{y}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018b8a08-b6bf-5dc7-8fe4-92c8ecc3ef70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384705} {:answer/id #uuid "c52a6769-f25d-5aba-9ab2-99be1a5ff558" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384706} {:answer/id #uuid "a6e22e8c-9c7a-550b-a6d5-cd39a1eb593a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384707} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e8c89d-dfe8-501a-bf65-95c3c6baa8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384708} {:answer/id #uuid "3648bd95-7162-5076-ac98-667f5353a545" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e8c89d-dfe8-501a-bf65-95c3c6baa8c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384708} :answer-field/id #uuid "be23ae1d-c459-5973-9102-bf6e3310a126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ae578e5d-9060-5ab0-8bf3-d4a37a35851c" :question/math-academy-id "q-164529" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=\\sqrt{3}Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,\\sqrt{3}Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384710 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d76586-45e3-5b3b-85dd-b06fb61affb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384712} {:answer/id #uuid "7b3fd534-6296-5312-bf32-41594889caf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384713} {:answer/id #uuid "1ddfbc20-fb88-5b94-a62d-b98f81820201" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384714} {:answer/id #uuid "1ebdb291-9059-58b5-a0b5-906a76e2a088" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384715} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5bce58-6411-568b-829e-bd203d7a3ea3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b3fd534-6296-5312-bf32-41594889caf0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384713} :answer-field/id #uuid "45c215f3-6d89-5f67-9643-3ba3fb00f492" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3674b555-a69b-5ab1-887c-aa586c058827" :question/math-academy-id "q-164536" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,2X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "328ebcf7-1142-5ac2-aa40-22a0a74a4e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384719} {:answer/id #uuid "bffc4c57-ddca-5453-84c3-0fe04bb25902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384720} {:answer/id #uuid "b77356bd-a7c4-5ebb-a001-12c52e15d793" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384721} {:answer/id #uuid "1b5f67d7-e6af-5b58-a116-e7a1ada1d280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384722} {:answer/id #uuid "020dd5c5-796c-5b7c-9c37-cb18499b2254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384723}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "020dd5c5-796c-5b7c-9c37-cb18499b2254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384723} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6917248-de85-5455-9b75-7282feb004f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c506193a-85c6-5094-962b-7c03d5841210" :question/math-academy-id "q-164538" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨\\text{_____},\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Y,\\,y=Z,\\,z=5X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Y,Z,5X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z,\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{r}^{2},\\,5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$  \n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384724 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477cf5a074" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Polar Parametrization of a Paraboloid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac4573f7-5442-5de8-bc37-b2d60d727eb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384726} {:answer/id #uuid "a0a90247-2195-5150-97bd-3184aa465e82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384727} {:answer/id #uuid "0626cb92-a43a-5bdc-baac-5d39f1b31a93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384728} {:answer/id #uuid "26502d6f-2334-5f6f-80d9-196639647c77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384729} {:answer/id #uuid "de0eb1b2-2aeb-5ded-b979-0cd267672d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de0eb1b2-2aeb-5ded-b979-0cd267672d54" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384730} :answer-field/id #uuid "03a12b12-d2a1-53b8-a55f-0b66b60709e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e29c164b-752c-5fa5-bd77-ca5f63d2912a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164539" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(r,\\theta ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(r,\\theta )=⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the elliptic paraboloid defined in Cartesian coordinates as\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=3X,\\,z=2Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,3X,2Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,3r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ef56d-869d-5939-9a0a-84477c