e transformations\n\n$$\nx=Z,\\,y=2X,\\,z=Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get \n\n$$\nZ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & ={X}^{2}+{Y}^{2} \\\\ Z & =(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ Z & ={r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,2X,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨Z,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{r}^{2},\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of Two Sheets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8337944b-8c6b-5850-aa78-5e2ba1cab123" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384733} {:answer/id #uuid "023d32d1-9b35-5912-9290-581063d32f8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384734} {:answer/id #uuid "b86faf07-5b74-5517-b0b1-c577f8babdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384735} {:answer/id #uuid "f77263aa-d712-5b4e-8f13-2752be640d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384736} {:answer/id #uuid "60ce3a1e-02c5-5b06-86af-d33a9c3d5238" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384737}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b86faf07-5b74-5517-b0b1-c577f8babdc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384735} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f567e6b-fc31-5bf9-b179-3a9538182aad" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d57d61e-c022-5e47-b2f8-6ee0ab10326a" :question/math-academy-id "q-164570" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\text{_____},\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the first component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-25 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3640813a-71fe-5578-aa82-828a0f424e6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384740} {:answer/id #uuid "1277e302-065d-582f-b571-2354fde522a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384741} {:answer/id #uuid "809e03c9-321d-5214-8d69-e6ef561f20cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384742} {:answer/id #uuid "a638c0db-8848-5dce-87a3-407ff7a77c53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384743} {:answer/id #uuid "36533be6-207c-590c-b938-5bb8c64f9a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36533be6-207c-590c-b938-5bb8c64f9a3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384744} :answer-field/id #uuid "537831a2-9eb2-5a67-8f96-88ce1307c42d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384739}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57c63c10-3e68-518b-8861-8c91cfafda62" :question/math-academy-id "q-164571" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,y,\\theta )$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{z}^{2}=25$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,z⟩=⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of Two Sheets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b39f4a9-f6d2-56b9-924e-8172c150b7ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384747} {:answer/id #uuid "5cb6f395-3a76-55cb-945a-4de53a45c930" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384748} {:answer/id #uuid "ac41a8d6-6c9d-57de-864a-8971e5fda4bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384749} {:answer/id #uuid "97c2a13d-cdfd-57bb-81c0-d80d5af6dfbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384750} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cf0be2-d20b-5fc5-87d7-f8de8cf47691" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384751}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97c2a13d-cdfd-57bb-81c0-d80d5af6dfbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384750} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b63eb85-2bf4-547a-a264-831e7ca376bf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384746}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "500cb2cd-2289-5d53-9343-5bb7400db3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-164576" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2}=-16.$ By expressing the equation of the hyperboloid in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "If $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(x,y,z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{z}^{2} & =-16 \\\\ {z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=16$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $x$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨z,r⟩=⟨4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of Two Sheets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f66ea3f-7bab-59c4-a415-a1d2f85eaafe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384754} {:answer/id #uuid "80c433fc-5352-50af-a5e5-b99a37f6ffa8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384755} {:answer/id #uuid "f94ae687-61cc-5aad-aca0-5dfc633291d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384756} {:answer/id #uuid "e7e422ef-0a58-5588-aa70-8670d6c37fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384757} {:answer/id #uuid "a7961ed3-054d-5aca-be4b-eb1c293cd1ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f94ae687-61cc-5aad-aca0-5dfc633291d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384756} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a9f88d1-1c2d-5f18-aa89-cdb721957858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17011128-98c3-5fd8-a5d1-56cd54176baf" :question/math-academy-id "q-164580" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{3}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=3Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=4X,\\,y=3Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{3}\\,Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4X,3Z,\\sqrt{3}\\,Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3Z,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384759 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of Two Sheets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a01a4e0-2a7f-5b87-8449-9c7678ef63d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384761} {:answer/id #uuid "219bd5d1-c90c-5cd1-b854-d34cdc4ed7eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384762} {:answer/id #uuid "72d91634-e4a4-5e81-93ed-06b612f16f84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384763} {:answer/id #uuid "89eb5ffc-273b-5a65-b9a5-ecba76f77b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384764} {:answer/id #uuid "6e05263a-f9d8-57ec-a811-1a4a37ea7ff1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384765}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89eb5ffc-273b-5a65-b9a5-ecba76f77b2d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384764} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6da799e-54ff-56a2-a303-ef67bbef8c4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7dbeb7e-e0a8-5864-a21a-43fc2742343e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164581" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}-{z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=5X,\\,y=2Y,\\,z=Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5X,2Y,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c9afe1b-7297-5cbd-bd8c-04a274455af6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384768} {:answer/id #uuid "cfae30f6-e3ff-51ae-a857-a4b986f30671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384769} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f8418b-7c40-5ea8-93cc-a16580d37ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384770} {:answer/id #uuid "aa55d33b-0b4e-5c68-bde6-fd9620a128bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384771} {:answer/id #uuid "86af6898-5636-57b6-a482-1aa60255c30a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1f8418b-7c40-5ea8-93cc-a16580d37ea6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384770} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d6aa9a6-9b27-5cb3-af88-3320eb70f4a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88515a94-949c-507f-88df-7d773b910b2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-164585" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=6Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{2}Y\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=6Z,\\,z=\\sqrt{2}Y\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,6Z,\\sqrt{2}Y⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6Z,\\,\\sqrt{2}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,6\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dece6a5a-e9b2-5531-85f0-3774d0e3f966" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of Two Sheets"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc42e11b-738d-59db-a705-95b01c96202c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384775} {:answer/id #uuid "628ab19e-6071-5f65-82ff-dca0ebe8b2f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384776} {:answer/id #uuid "f753ff7d-3372-5961-88e4-68fdf89b0740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384777} {:answer/id #uuid "d10641bb-cf35-5fcb-9db3-7d42dcac5afa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384778} {:answer/id #uuid "0d7343ee-4247-58ee-8800-7045ab09f720" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384779}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f753ff7d-3372-5961-88e4-68fdf89b0740" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384777} :answer-field/id #uuid "b494e6e3-f12a-5a77-86e3-1ea3895fc4a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f5d21c2-71d4-58f8-813e-9499f9fe949d" :question/math-academy-id "q-164586" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____}⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes one sheet of the hyperboloid of two sheets defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Z,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the third component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨Z,r⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=3Z,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2X\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=-1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =-1 \\\\ {Z}^{2}-{r}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${Z}^{2}-{r}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨Z,r⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Z,4Y,2X⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3Z,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2(\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the third component of $f$ is $2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10bfbafe-b8d7-57d8-9dcb-b84b85328620" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Hyperboloid of One Sheet"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ca693f-d8e4-5ba9-8a63-930e8ef273c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384782} {:answer/id #uuid "1632df54-a340-58ea-98ae-9f8b8329aa8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384783} {:answer/id #uuid "23e35e70-83cf-5174-a46b-4cd26652633f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384784} {:answer/id #uuid "1262a4ee-0fbe-53e3-bd82-adb45acd6cbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384785} {:answer/id #uuid "56f7210a-838c-567e-92d0-2d48a78e5a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1632df54-a340-58ea-98ae-9f8b8329aa8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384783} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9807320-b585-5178-b855-c151dea035ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88010eb3-1670-5c7e-9a7d-7a10ea663604" :question/math-academy-id "q-164587" :question/problem "The vector-valued function $f(\\theta ,ϕ),$ given by \n\n$$\nf(\\theta ,ϕ)=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\text{_____},\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\n$$\n\nparametrizes the hyperboloid of one sheet defined in Cartesian coordinates as \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$\n\nBy first considering the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2Z\n$$\n\nand writing the resulting system in cylindrical polar coordinates, determine the second component of $f.$\n\n*Hint: You may use the fact that the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}={k}^{2}$ can be parametrized as $⟨r,Z⟩=⟨k\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,k\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩$* for $ϕ\\in R.$" :question/worked-solution "First, substituting the transformations\n\n$$\nx=X,\\,y=4Y,\\,z=2Z\n$$\n\ninto our equation, we get\n\n$$\n{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,Z)$ are the cylindrical coordinates of a point and $(X,Y,Z)$ are the corresponding Cartesian coordinates of that point, then we have\n\n$$\nX=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,Y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,Z=Z.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}^{2}+{Y}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-{Z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {r}^{2}-{Z}^{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the hyperbola ${r}^{2}-{Z}^{2}=1$ represents the intersection of our hyperboloid with the plane that passes through the $Z$-axis and makes the angle of $\\theta $ with the positive $X$-axis. Using the hint, a parametrization of such hyperbola can be given as\n\n$$\n⟨r,Z⟩=⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩,\\,ϕ\\in R.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨X,4Y,2Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2Z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ and $ϕ\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}]}
